awk is a powerful Linux command-line utility used for searching, filtering, processing, and formatting text. It is especially useful for working with structured text files where data is organized into columns.
It processes input line by line and field by field.
Why do we use awk?
Print specific columns from a file.
Search for patterns.
Filter data.
Perform calculations.
Generate formatted reports.
Process log files.
Syntax  awk 'pattern { action }' filename
awk '{print $1}' student.txt   awk '{print $1, $3}' student.txt  awk '{print NR, $0}' student.txt  awk '$3 > 80 {print $2, $3}' student.txt  awk '/Siva/' student.txt

Output:
101
102
103
Variable	Meaning
$1	First column
$2	Second column
$3	Third column
$NF	Last column
$0	Entire line
NR	Current line number
NF	Number of fields in the current line



